	"_comment": "Lietuvių (Lithuanian). Composed done-shape is Subject + passive past participle with elided copula — the register a Lithuanian status line actually uses: 'Failas ištrintas', 'Užduotis ištrinta' (not 'Failas yra ištrintas'). Modern Lithuanian has TWO genders (m/f) — no neuter — and no articles (article.none). All 18 verbs end their masculine participle in -as, so feminine agreement is one mechanical rule from the masculine base — {strip: 's'} (ištrintas → ištrinta) — verified individually against every entry below; no per-verb past_f overrides needed. 'push' is deliberately DROPPED: the literal verb (stumti/pastumti, 'to shove') is a poor semantic fit for git-push, and its -imas verbal noun would need a root-ablaut (stum- → stūm-, as in kulti/kūlimas) I could not verify with confidence — a gap declared rather than guessed. The gerund slot is the verbal-noun progress register real Lithuanian software shows (Trynimas..., Kūrimas...); 'find' pairs the resultative rasti/rastas with the SEARCH verb's noun ieškojimas for progress, since perfective 'find' has no natural progressive — the same imperfective-partner move pl.json documents for wypychanie/znajdowanie. 'build' takes the established noun 'statyba' over the stricter derivation 'statymas'; 'start' takes 'pradžia' (a beginning) over the stiffer 'pradėjimas' — both the same plain-deverbative choice sl.json makes for začetek. 'commit' maps to patvirtinti ('confirm/approve'), a semantic stand-in for the git sense, the same adaptation Polish makes with zatwierdzić. 'message' uses pranešimas (formal notification/error-message register) over žinutė (personal text message). testuoti and įdiegti are standard dictionary IT terms, not slang. žmogus → žmonės is a genuine suppletive irregular plural. repozitorija (the -ija loanword class of versija/kategorija) is authored with slightly lower confidence — a reasonable calque, not a headword I have dictionary-verified. NOMINATIVE-only composition, declared deliberately: real Lithuanian puts a transitive infinitive's object in the ACCUSATIVE, and negation (as 'Nepavyko...' is) shifts it further to the GENITIVE of negation — neither is modelled, so the fail-sentence object and the counted noun both surface in plain nominative. Plural resolution is binary (one/other); Lithuanian's numeral-governed singular-after-21-type patterns are not modelled, the same simplification pl.json and sl.json declare for their own languages.",
